{"name":"Chinese Porcelain","hex":"#3a5f7d","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"porcelain","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A hard white translucent ceramic, originally made by firing kaolin, quartz, and feldspar at high temperatures but now also inclusive of similar artificial materials; also often  such a material as a symbol of the fragility, elegance, etc. traditionally associated with porcelain goods.","Synonym of china: porcelain tableware."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To coat with a porcelain enamel."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/porcelain"}],"entries":[{"title":"Chinese ceramics","blocks":[{"text":"Chinese ceramics are one of the most significant forms of Chinese art and ceramics globally. They range from construction materials such as bricks and tiles, to hand-built pottery vessels fired in bonfires or kilns, to the sophisticated Chinese porcelain wares made for the imperial court and for export."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/%E9%9D%92%E7%A3%81%E7%9B%A4_Dish%2C_Celadon_glaze.jpg/330px-%E9%9D%92%E7%A3%81%E7%9B%A4_Dish%2C_Celadon_glaze.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chinese_ceramics"},{"title":"Porcelain","blocks":[{"text":"Porcelain, also called china, is a ceramic material made by heating raw materials, generally including kaolinite, in a kiln to temperatures between 1,200 and 1,400 °C. The greater strength and translucence of porcelain, relative to other types of pottery, arise mainly from vitrification and the formation of the mineral mullite within the body at these high temperatures. End applications include tableware, decorative ware such as figurines, and products in technology and industry such as electrical insulators and laboratory ware."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9b/%E6%B1%9D%E7%AA%AF_%E9%9D%92%E7%93%B7%E8%86%BD%E7%93%B6.png/330px-%E6%B1%9D%E7%AA%AF_%E9%9D%92%E7%93%B7%E8%86%BD%E7%93%B6.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Porcelain"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/%E8%89%AF%E6%B8%9A%E6%96%87%E5%8C%96%E5%88%BB%E7%AC%A6%E9%99%B6%E7%89%87%2C_2021-12-04_02.jpg/500px-%E8%89%AF%E6%B8%9A%E6%96%87%E5%8C%96%E5%88%BB%E7%AC%A6%E9%99%B6%E7%89%87%2C_2021-12-04_02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/%E8%89%AF%E6%B8%9A%E6%96%87%E5%8C%96%E5%88%BB%E7%AC%A6%E9%99%B6%E7%89%87%2C_2021-12-04_02.jpg/1280px-%E8%89%AF%E6%B8%9A%E6%96%87%E5%8C%96%E5%88%BB%E7%AC%A6%E9%99%B6%E7%89%87%2C_2021-12-04_02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/%E8%89%AF%E6%B8%9A%E6%96%87%E5%8C%96%E5%88%BB%E7%AC%A6%E9%99%B6%E7%89%87%2C_2021-12-04_02.jpg/500px-%E8%89%AF%E6%B8%9A%E6%96%87%E5%8C%96%E5%88%BB%E7%AC%A6%E9%99%B6%E7%89%87%2C_2021-12-04_02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Pottery shard with incised symbols, Liangzhu culture (c. 3300–2300 BCE).","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A%E8%89%AF%E6%B8%9A%E6%96%87%E5%8C%96%E5%88%BB%E7%AC%A6%E9%99%B6%E7%89%87%2C_2021-12-04_02.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Porcelain_Production_Diorama_%2810107008074%29.jpg/500px-Porcelain_Production_Diorama_%2810107008074%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Porcelain_Production_Diorama_%2810107008074%29.jpg/1280px-Porcelain_Production_Diorama_%2810107008074%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Porcelain_Production_Diorama_%2810107008074%29.